          "source_quote": "τοῦ κυβερνήτου μόνου τοὺς λόγους ἀντιβαλλέτω",
          "english_quote": "should relay the words of the helmsman alone",
          "issue": "“Relay” settles the action as onward transmission of the helmsman’s words. The responsive or corresponding force of ἀντιβαλλέτω needs examination: answering, echoing, or matching the helmsman’s words is not necessarily the same action as relaying them. The draft’s lexical note explains the attachment of μόνου but does not justify this verb rendering or disclose its interpretive uncertainty. This is an unresolved meaning question, not evidence of corruption in the image-checked Greek.",
          "suggested_revision": "Examine attested uses of ἀντιβάλλω with λόγους, particularly command-response contexts. A provisional candidate for fresh review is “should respond to the words of the helmsman alone,” with “echo/repeat the words” retained as an alternative if supported. Record the unresolved verb sense in source_issues without proposing an alteration to the supplied Greek.",
          "severity": "major"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "ὁ χριστὸς ὡς σωτὴρ κυβερνήτης φιλείσθω",
          "english_quote": "The Messiah, as Savior and helmsman, should be loved",
          "issue": "The Greek places σωτήρ and κυβερνήτης together under one ὡς, without a coordinating καί. “Savior and helmsman” is a possible interpretation, but the draft presents that coordination as settled. It does not disclose the closer combined designation “savior-helmsman” or the possible descriptive reading “saving helmsman.” The existing rationale about preserving both images does not resolve their grammatical relationship.",
          "suggested_revision": "Compare “The Messiah, as the savior-helmsman, should be loved” as a source-transparent candidate for fresh review. Preserve “as Savior and helmsman” and “as the saving helmsman” as interpretive alternatives in the note rather than certifying one relationship as unambiguous.",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
